href="lambek-1958">[lambek-1958]</fr:link></fr:meta></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter><fr:p>Lambek's <fr:em>syntactic calculus</fr:em> comprise the following rules:

<fr:ul><fr:li><fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[x \to  x]]></fr:tex></fr:li>
  <fr:li><fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[(xy) z \to  x (yz)]]></fr:tex></fr:li>
  <fr:li><fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[x (y z) \to  (xy) z]]></fr:tex></fr:li>
  <fr:li>if <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[xy \to  z]]></fr:tex> then <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[x \to  z/y]]></fr:tex></fr:li>
  <fr:li>if <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[xy \to  z]]></fr:tex> then <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[y \to  x \backslash  z]]></fr:tex></fr:li>
  <fr:li>if <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[x \to  z/y]]></fr:tex> then <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[xy \to  z]]></fr:tex></fr:li>
  <fr:li>if <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[y \to  x \backslash  z]]></fr:tex> then <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[xy \to  z]]></fr:tex></fr:li>
  <fr:li>if <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[x \to  y]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[y \to  z]]></fr:tex> then <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[x \to  z]]></fr:tex></fr:li></fr:ul></fr:p><fr:p>Put by Lambek:
<fr:blockquote>The system presented above may be viewed abstractly as a formal language with a number of primitive type symbols...</fr:blockquote></fr:p><fr:p>This description rings true, as we can read this as a very simple for type system for grammars that is later made more precise by <fr:link
type="local"
href="dougherty-1993.xml"
addr="dougherty-1993"
title="Closed Categories and Categorial Grammar">Dougherty</fr:link>.</fr:p></fr:mainmatter><fr:backmatter><fr:tree
